 Newgate", "domain": "en.wikipedia.org", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"}], "_description_provenance": {"v": 1, "ai": "selected", "run": "v3-2026-09-26", "card": null, "lane": "S", "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "sources": [{"id": "W", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Newgate&oldid=1263631171", "revid": 1263631171, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "text_sha256": "b3e0edcb1ae11cba9ca729b8487519fbe6cc5dd88b1e6d26c7d47a88af448680", "rev_timestamp": "2024-12-17T19:53:12Z"}], "ai_system": "Claude Opus (Anthropic): anthropic/claude-opus-5-5 (Claude Code agent), an-sites-remediation-2026-09", "sentences": [{"n": 1, "end": 139, "src": "W", "drop": [], "start": 0}, {"n": 1, "end": 271, "src": "W", "drop": [], "start": 140}, {"n": 1, "end": 394, "src": "W", "drop": [], "start": 272}], "attribution": {"url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Newgate&oldid=1263631171", "title": "Newgate", "changes": "sentences selected and shortened", "licence_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}, "desc_sha256": "0ffca32de7b8d64919f963c2f5a3375a144d0058fd9dff16caf5bc8d00168ace"}}'' restored on Newgate', '0ffca32de7b8d64919f963c2f5a3375a144d0058fd9dff16caf5bc8d00168ace', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-write-014", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Newgate, one of the six London Wall gates dating back to Roman times, sent its road west over the River Fleet. From the 12th century parts of its buildings held a gaol, later Newgate Prison."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-014 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Newgate was one of the London Wall gates [S1]; it was one of the six gates dating back to Roman times [S1]; its road headed west over the River Fleet [S2]; from the 12th century parts of its buildings were used as a gaol [S3]; the gaol later developed into Newgate Prison [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-042 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Newgate was a gate of the London Wall (the wall around the City of London) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newgate]; Newgate stood on the site of a gate in the Roman London Wall (second source for the central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://www.londonmuseum.org.uk/collections/london-stories/history-londons-newgate-prison/]; Newgate was one of the six London Wall gates dating back to Roman times [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newgate]; The Roman-era gates were Ludgate, Newgate, Cripplegate, Bishopsgate and Aldgate, with Aldersgate added around 350; Moorgate is medieval (which makes six Roman-era gates including Newgate) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_Wall]; Newgate lay on the west side of the wall and its road went west over the River Fleet [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newgate]; From the 12th century parts of the gate buildings were used as a gaol [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newgate]; One of the Newgate turrets, which still served as a main gate into the city, was converted into an extension of the prison (the gate buildings and the gaol shared the site) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newgate_Prison]; The gaol later developed into Newgate Prison [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newgate]; Newgate Prison was in use from 1188 to the early twentieth century [SUPPORTED: https://www.londonmuseum.org.uk/collections/london-stories/history-londons-newgate-prison/]"}]'::jsonb),
